  "raw_table_text": "Table 1. Baseline characteristics of study participants by area of residence (n = 300).\nCharacteristic\nHigh-Traffic Area (n = 150)\nResidential Area (n = 150)\np-value\nAge, years — mean ± SD\n34.6 ± 9.2\n33.9 ± 8.7\n0.48\nSex\nMale\n72 (48.0%)\n73 (48.7%)\n0.90\nFemale\n78 (52.0%)\n77 (51.3%)\nEducation Level\nPrimary/secondary\n61 (40.7%)\n58 (38.7%)\n0.71\nTertiary\n89 (59.3%)\n92 (61.3%)\nEmployment status\nEmployed\n118 (78.7%)\n114 (76.0%)\n0.57\nUnemployed/student\n32 (21.3%)\n36 (24.0%)\nYears of residence-mean ± SD\n7.4 ± 4.1\n6.9 ± 3.8\n0.27\nNoise exposure\nMean LAeq,24h (dBA) ± SD\n74.6 ± 6.8\n52.3 ± 5.1\n<0.001\nMean difference (95% CI)\n22.3 (20.9–23.7)\n–\n<0.001\nHigh exposure >65 dBA, n (%)\n117 (78.0%)\n33 (22.0%)\n<0.001\nPsychosocial measures\nPSS-10 Mean ± SD\n22.4 ± 5.3\n14.8 ± 4.7\n<0.001\nHigh stress (PSS-10 ≥ 20), n (%)\n98 (65.3%)\n41 (27.3%)\n<0.001\nPSQI mean ± SD\n8.1 ± 3.2\n4.6 ± 2.8\n<0.001\nPoor sleep (PSQI >5), n (%)\n104 (69.3%)\n52 (34.7%)\n<0.001\nData presented as n (%) or mean ± SD. p-values from independent samples t-test (continuous) or Pearson chi-square (categorical). PSS-10 = Perceived Stress Scale-10; PSQI = Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index; BMI = body mass index; LAeq,24h = energy-averaged 24-hour noise level.\nTable 2\ndisplays descriptive statistics for all clinical oral health outcomes by study group. On all assessed outcomes, individuals residing near high traffic experienced significantly worse oral health. TMJ problems were reported by 61.3% of those assigned to the high-traffic group compared to 25.3% of low traffic area residents (OR = 4.68; 95% CI: 2.84–7.71; p < 0.001). On the Helkimo severity grading scale, higher grades indicative of worsening TMJ dysfunction were progressively more frequent in the high-traffic group, χ\n2\n= 40.50, p < 0.001, such that moderate and severe dysfunction combined was seen in 32.0% of high traffic participants compared with 10.6% assigned to reside near low traffic (χ\n2\n= 40.50; p < 0.001). Malocclusion was twice as frequent in the high-traffic group (52.0% vs. 26.0%; χ\n2\n= 21.31; p < 0.001) and bruxism was also significantly more prevalent (42.7% vs. 14.0%; χ\n2\n= 30.35; p < 0.001). Median TMJ pain intensity was also significantly greater in those assigned to the high-traffic group (median VAS 4 [IQR 2–7] vs. 0 [IQR 0–3]; effect size r = 0.46; p = 0.001), a clinically meaningful difference that approached the threshold for moderate pain.\n"
